?end
-> :night

# morning
## The twenty first of Brumaire
You are in the haven before the day, with the stone over you, and you hear nothing of it. The Tribunal sits at eight. It is in the great hall of the Palace, which is the room in which the kings of France were once crowned with a great deal of ceremony, and it is a thing that people say, now, with a lowered voice, that it is a fine room for it.
It is told you at dusk by Baptiste, who comes down the stair himself, in his shirt sleeves, without his hat, with a very white face, and stands at the foot with one hand on the wall.
He says that the three were tried at ten. He says that it was done in a quarter of an hour, and that the clerk and the lace seller said nothing at all, and that the Chevalier was asked whether he had anything to say. He says that the Chevalier said that he had only one observation, which was that the court was a great deal more interesting than he had been led to expect. He says that they laughed again. He says that the sentence was passed at a little after two, and that the three were taken to the Conciergerie to be made ready.
He says that it had been supposed that it would be done at four. He says that the cart was kept, because the Tribunal had another case, and then a third, and that at half past four it was not yet out, and that by five the light was going and that it was in the Place that torches were lit.
?if b3.committee == "served"
  He says that it has been remarked, in the shop, that it is a pity about the new lists, since the old one was so well set.
?end
?if b3.yard_veil == "let"
  He says that there was a girl at the Pont au Change, in a gray dress, with a stack of hand bills, who was stopped by the Guard, and then not stopped, and that she has gone down towards the Place with a great many young people in her train, in the fog, and has not been seen.
?elif b3.yard_veil == "sent"
  He says that there are six young men at the front of the crowd, in plain coats, who have the look of persons who are about to see a thing that they have been told of, and do not like it.
?end
He says that he thought that you would wish to know.
-> place

# place
## The Place of the Revolution
You go on foot, along the river, in the last of the dusk, and the fog comes up from the Seine in rags. The whole of the Place is full. It is not to be believed. It is a quarter of a mile of heads, with the horses of the Guard in lines, and the lamps lit upon poles, and the torches. They are tarred and burning, in iron cressets, round the base of the machine, and they send up a heavy red smoke, that hangs over the whole of the square at the height of a roof. You find a place under the colonnade of the Garde Meuble, against a pillar, in a hat, in the thick of the dark, where a man may stand without being looked at. Nobody does.
There is a drum. It is an ugly sound. It is a good deal slower than it should be. The cart comes in by the rue Royale, in the smoke, with the three of them in it, sitting up with their hands tied behind them. The clerk is gray. The woman has her eyes shut. Brézac is in the plain gray coat, bare headed, with the scar pale in the torchlight, and he is looking over the heads of the crowd, with great attention, at something in the middle distance. He finds it. It is you. His face does not change. He inclines his head, a quarter of an inch, as a man does at the opera, to an acquaintance in a box.
?if b3.cell == "promised"
  You have kept your word. It is a poor thing, in the circumstances, and it is all that there is.
?end
?if b3.marais == "vowed"
  There is a plain coach under the trees by the Tuileries wall, with the blinds down, and Ottaviano on the box. It has been standing there since dusk.
?elif b3.marais == "pressed"
  There is a plain coach under the trees by the Tuileries wall, with the blinds drawn. The door has opened. There is a hand on the sill, white, very small, that does not seem able to decide whether to come out. It does not.
?end
?if b3.yard_veil == "let"
  Clémence is on the steps of the Ministry of the Marine, on a barrel, in the gray dress, and has the hand bills in a heap at her feet. She is quite calm. She is waiting.
?elif b3.yard_veil == "sent"
  Severin is at the front, between two of the National Guard, in a plain coat. He has not got a hand bill. He is looking at the machine.
?end
?if b3.officer == "pledged"
  The Senate's man is at the edge of the colonnade, under a lamp, with his gloves. He has the little notebook open. He does not look at you.
?elif b3.officer == "defied"
  The Senate's man is at the edge of the colonnade, in a good coat, and a hat. He has brought two others. They are quite still. It is not at all a matter of pretending that you have not seen them.
?else
  The Senate's man is at the edge of the colonnade, under a lamp, with his gloves, and he has a small book.
?end
They are taken in order. The lace seller is first. She is quick, and does not make a sound. The clerk is the second. He says that it is a mistake, in a high voice, to the crowd, and the crowd does not say anything. It is done in the time that it takes to lift a hat.
It is the third. He mounts the steps by himself, and does not take the arm of the assistant. He stands. He looks, for the last time, at the whole of the Place, and the fog, and the torches. He says, in a clear, light, carrying voice, that is not at all like a speech, that he would be obliged if somebody would see to the horse. He is laid on the plank. The plank goes forward. There is a click, and the whole of the Place is absolutely still.
The knife comes down. It makes the sound that it always makes, which is smaller than you were afraid. The assistant takes the head out of the basket, by the hair, and walks to the rail, and holds it up. The roar begins, in the front, and goes back, in a wave, through the whole of the Place. It is not an angry sound. It is a sound that is made by a very large number of persons who are all glad to be somebody else.
It stops. It does not stop altogether. It stops at the front. The assistant is looking at the head in his hand. It has opened its eyes.
It looks at the crowd, with perfect gravity, and then, for an instant, directly at you. The mouth moves. It says one word, without a voice, that you could read from any distance in the world. It says: now.
The assistant lets go of it. It falls on the boards, and rolls, and the front of the crowd gives back like water. The body on the plank has moved. It has put out a hand. It is a large, fair, white hand, with the knuckles scarred, and it feels along the boards, with a patient, practical air, like the hand of a man who has dropped his glove. It finds the head. It lifts it. There is a perfectly clear and audible sound in the silence, like the closing of a very well made box.
Brézac sits up on the plank of the guillotine, with his head on his shoulders, and the scar on his face, and puts up his hand to the place at his neck, and looks about him with a mild, rather puzzled, courteous interest, as one who has been wakened in a strange house.
Nobody breathes. It is a thing that lasts, perhaps, four seconds. It is a very long time.
* Make a cry, in the crowd, that turns every head
  $ b3.rising = "covered"
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ secrecy += 3
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_twenty_first_of_brumaire")
  You do it from the pillar, in the old voice that carries without being loud, and in the fine, high, shrill accents of the women of the Halles. You cry that it is the Austrians. You cry that they have broken in at the Barrière. You cry it twice. It goes through the crowd like a draught through a house. A thousand heads go round at once, and a thousand people begin to run, and there is a crash and a scream by the Ministry of the Marine, and a horse goes down, and the Guard shouts. It is a hideous noise. It is the best that can be done. By the time it is quiet the machine has been cleared, and the assistant is sitting on the steps, with his head between his knees, and the three bodies have been put in the cart, and the Place is going home. Two persons, it is said, were trodden to death at the Garde Meuble. You do not ask for their names.
* Go through the crowd, and take him out in the dark
  $ b3.rising = "carried"
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ defiance += 2
  $ humanity += 1
  $ standing += 1
  $ bond.brezac += 3
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_twenty_first_of_brumaire")
  You go across the Place at the pace of a man who is going to give evidence. Nobody stops you. The assistants have got off the platform and are standing in the smoke with their mouths open. You go up the steps. You say, in a loud flat voice, in the voice of the Section, that the citizen is not dead, and that he is to be taken in charge, and is to be examined, on the order of the Committee, and you say a name. It is the name of a man in a round hat, and it is a very good name. You get an arm round him. He is very heavy, and quite cold. He leans on you, like a drunk man, with a sort of formal apology. The wicker coffin, in the cart, is the nearest, and you put him into it, and the lid goes down, and a plain coach comes slowly through the crowd to the foot of the steps, with its blinds down, and takes it.
* Do nothing, and stand under the pillar, and look
  $ b3.rising = "watched"
  $ secrecy -= 4
  $ humanity += 1
  $ defiance += 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_twenty_first_of_brumaire")
  You do not move. It is a decision, and it is the only one that has ever seemed to you to be wholly honest. You watch the whole of the thing. He gets to his feet. He is quite steady. He says, in the same clear light voice, that he is very much obliged to the Section, and that there appears to have been some mistake. There is a long sound. It is not a cry. It is a sound that is made by a very great number of persons all at once, who are exactly as frightened as they have ever been. A woman begins to pray. Somebody laughs, and goes on laughing. It goes on, in waves. The Guard has not got an order. It is some while before anybody is able to think of one. By that time several hundred persons have seen it, and there is not very much to be done about it.
It is dark. The torches are burning down. The Place is not at all quiet.
The hand of the Lady is moving, you know, a long way off, in the dark of a cellar, on a slate. It is writing one word.
$ do rest()
